            {
              "id": "c1a4eaa8-48df-33c3-b7c2-fd9ccca7ea32",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur in the C function `strlen()`. This function is used to determine the length of a string, but it does not perform any bounds checking. If an attacker can control the input to `strlen()`, they can cause it to read beyond the end of the intended buffer, potentially leading to a crash or code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `sprintf()` is used to append a string to `(*kv)[*kvnum].name`. The length of the original string is determined using `strlen()`, and the new string is written starting at that position. If the original string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` could return a value larger than the actual buffer size, leading to a buffer overflow when `sprintf()` tries to write the new string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should ensure that all strings are properly null-terminated before passing them to `strlen()`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `sprintf()`, such as `snprintf()`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to be written to the output string, including the null character. This can prevent buffer overflows if used correctly.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```c++\nchar *name = (*kv)[*kvnum].name;\nsize_t len = strlen(name);\nif (len < sizeof((*kv)[*kvnum].name)) {\n    snprintf(name + len, sizeof((*kv)[*kvnum].name) - len, \".smn[%\" PRId64 \"]\", cur_smn);\n}\n```\n\nThis code first checks if the length of the original string is less than the buffer size before attempting to append the new string. If the original string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will return a value equal to or larger than the buffer size, and the `snprintf()` call will be skipped.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `sprintf()` and `snprintf()`\n- `string.h` for `strlen()`\n- `inttypes.h` for `PRId64`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eb2cd348-d530-3f57-93ab-407dc81c48a7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur in the C function `strlen()`. This function is used to determine the length of a string, but it does not perform any bounds checking. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading until it encounters a null byte, potentially leading to a buffer overflow. This can result in undefined behavior, including program crashes, data corruption, and even code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `sprintf()` is used to append a formatted string to the end of an existing string. However, if the size of the formatted string exceeds the remaining space in the destination buffer, a buffer overflow will occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo prevent buffer overflow vulnerabilities, it is important to always perform bounds checking before writing to a buffer. This can be done by ensuring that the size of the data being written does not exceed the size of the buffer.\n\nIn addition, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()` and `sprintf()`, such as `strnlen()` and `snprintf()`, which take an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of bytes to read or write.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t name_len = strnlen((*kv)[*kvnum].name, MAX_NAME_LEN);\nsnprintf((*kv)[*kvnum].name + name_len, MAX_NAME_LEN - name_len, \"[%\" PRId64 \"]\", y);\n```\n\nIn this fix, `strnlen()` is used to safely determine the length of the string, and `snprintf()` is used to prevent writing beyond the end of the buffer. `MAX_NAME_LEN` should be defined as the size of the `name` buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `sprintf()`\n- `string.h` for `strlen()`\n- `inttypes.h` for `PRId64`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b517e81f-579c-3f15-bf99-3de0628b5e03",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name (`fname`) is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `fopen`, consider using a function that allows for more secure handling of files. Always validate and sanitize input from untrusted sources. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream infile;\ninfile.open(fname, std::ios::binary);\n```\n\nIn this example, the C++ `ifstream` class is used to open the file in binary mode. This class provides more secure file handling than the C `fopen` function.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "24b32dbb-ee86-35a5-8821-37a76386e4ca",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name (`fname`) is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `fopen`, consider using a function that allows for more secure handling of files. Always validate and sanitize input from untrusted sources. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream infile;\ninfile.open(fname, std::ios::binary);\n```\n\nIn this example, the C++ `ifstream` class is used to open the file in binary mode. This class provides more secure file handling than the C `fopen` function.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fb30d248-8c61-3853-ad65-ad3708750aae",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory beyond the intended boundary, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This can result in crashes, incorrect behavior, or potential execution of malicious code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(info.container_name) > 0`, there is a potential risk if `info.container_name` is not a null-terminated string. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen()` on strings that may not be null-terminated. Instead, consider using safer alternatives such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter to specify the maximum length of the string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strlen()` function with `strnlen()`. The second parameter to `strnlen()` should be the maximum size of `info.container_name`. \n\n```cpp\nstrnlen(info.container_name, sizeof(info.container_name)) > 0\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed for the original code. The `strnlen()` function is also part of the C standard library, but it is not available in all environments. If `strnlen()` is not available, you may need to implement a similar function yourself.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7a0fbe1c-3fe0-3309-b9b3-151c1cb05f8e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `strcpy` and instead use safer string handling functions that include buffer size as a parameter, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by ensuring that no more than the specified number of bytes is written to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(info.container_name, container_id.c_str(), sizeof(info.container_name) - 1);\ninfo.container_name[sizeof(info.container_name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string from `container_id` to `info.container_name`, but it will not write more than `sizeof(info.container_name) - 1` bytes. The last byte is set to the null character to ensure that `info.container_name` is a null-terminated string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strncpy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "698121a6-d90d-3bac-a372-926809dbf2c7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(container_type_name[i])`, the vulnerability arises if `container_type_name[i]` is not a null-terminated string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen()`. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to read.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `container_type_name` is an array of strings, you can ensure null-termination as follows:\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure null-termination\ncontainer_type_name[i][sizeof(container_type_name[i]) - 1] = '\\0';\n\n// Now it's safe to call strlen\nsize_t len = strlen(container_type_name[i]);\n```\n\nIf you have control over the creation of the strings, ensure they are null-terminated at creation time.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e2e60ecd-2528-39f3-a361-6e72834934a6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path (`sysfs_path`) is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `fopen` such as `fopen_s` or `fstream`. These functions provide better error handling and are less prone to vulnerabilities. Additionally, always validate and sanitize input that will be used as a file path to prevent path traversal or other types of attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with `fstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(sysfs_path.c_str(), std::ios::binary);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a7cece13-1c56-3fdf-a0c9-f78ec0f12833",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the destination and source may overlap based on data flow, which can cause undefined behavior with memcpy. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the verdict is marked as genuine. Both the source and destination are confirmed to be non-null, so the main risk is the potential for overlapping memory regions, which memcpy does not handle safely.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, add a runtime check to ensure the source and destination do not overlap before calling `memcpy`. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead, which is safe for overlapping regions.\n\n**Example fix with explicit overlap check:**\n\n```cpp\nif ((p_binary_data < cache.data.data() + b_size) && (cache.data.data() < (char*)p_binary_data + b_size)) {\n    // Regions overlap, use memmove\n    std::memmove(p_binary_data, cache.data.data(), b_size);\n} else {\n    // No overlap, safe to use memcpy\n    std::memcpy(p_binary_data, cache.data.data(), b_size);\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, replace the `memcpy` call with `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. This change ensures correct behavior regardless of whether the source and destination overlap.\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(p_binary_data, cache.data.data(), b_size);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "06b1e788-5be4-3467-aaae-0a2c320ca462",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null, and there are no obvious null pointer checks before the buffer operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', confirming the high confidence in the issue. The source is a C++ string's c_str() method, which is generally safe, but the destination pointer may be null, as there is no evidence of a guard or check to ensure it is valid. The use of strncpy without a guarantee that the destination is non-null can lead to a crash or undefined behavior if a null pointer is passed. No explicit null-termination is performed after the copy, which is another risk, but the primary concern here is the lack of null pointer validation for the destination.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy operation, ensure that the destination buffer pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to prevent undefined behavior if the destination is null. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (info.name != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(info.name, name.c_str(), std::min((unsigned long)AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH, name.length()));\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination:\n    info.name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis prevents the function from attempting to write to a null pointer, which would otherwise cause a crash or undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the destination buffer is a fixed-size array and should never be null, ensure that it is always properly initialized before use. If it is a pointer, allocate memory before copying:\n\n```cpp\nif (info.name == nullptr) {\n    info.name = new char[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH];\n}\nstrncpy(info.name, name.c_str(), std::min((unsigned long)AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH, name.length()));\ninfo.name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\nThis ensures the destination buffer is valid and large enough for the copy operation.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5ae42019-828f-382e-8ef2-c543c110ffc1",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the data flow analysis. Additionally, the source pointer may be null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues, and the function verdict confirms this as a genuine issue. The risk is further increased because the code does not use a function that is safe for overlapping memory regions, nor does it validate the pointers before use.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add explicit checks before the memory copy operation to verify that the regions are distinct:\n\n```cpp\nif ((char*)&pmmetrics_version + 4 <= (char*)&buf1[12] || (char*)&buf1[12] + 4 <= (char*)&pmmetrics_version) {\n    memcpy(&pmmetrics_version, &buf1[12], 4);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case appropriately\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, handle it in a way that avoids undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions cannot be ruled out, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is safe for overlapping regions:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(&pmmetrics_version, &buf1[12], 4);\n```\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is well-defined even if the source and destination overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1e8a628d-b4db-3245-9bb3-f031f781c813",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The verdict is marked as 'GENUINE', and the classification explicitly states there is a risk of undefined behavior. The analysis detected that the source and destination may overlap based on data flow, which can cause issues with memcpy, as it does not handle overlapping memory safely. Additionally, there is a possibility that the source pointer could be null, which would also result in undefined behavior. No explicit bounds checks or guards are present to mitigate these risks. Therefore, this is a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to ensure memory safety.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, add a runtime check to ensure the source and destination do not overlap before calling the memory copy function. If overlap is possible, use a function that safely handles overlapping regions, such as `memmove`. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif ((reinterpret_cast<const char*>(header) + header->record_length <= reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&cper_data[data_idx])) ||\n    (reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&cper_data[data_idx]) + header->record_length <= reinterpret_cast<const char*>(header))) {\n    std::memcpy(&cper_data[data_idx], reinterpret_cast<const char*>(header), header->record_length);\n} else {\n    std::memmove(&cper_data[data_idx], reinterpret_cast<const char*>(header), header->record_length);\n}\n```\nThis approach ensures that `memcpy` is only used when it is safe, and `memmove` is used when there is a risk of overlap.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, replace the memory copy operation with `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely:\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(&cper_data[data_idx], reinterpret_cast<const char*>(header), header->record_length);\n```\nThis change eliminates the risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
