            {
              "id": "452bbef9-a323-3333-857f-2e7406dd6bdd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, instead of using `fopen`, you can use the `fstream` class from the C++ Standard Library which provides a higher level of abstraction and more control over file operations.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `fstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::string path_max_link_speed;\n// ... (initialize path_max_link_speed)\nstd::ifstream file(path_max_link_speed);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: for file operations\n- `<string>`: for string operations\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a85c9998-02cd-36ca-aeb9-d947e77f3af9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code snippet, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path is manipulated or controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `fopen`, consider using `fstream` objects from the C++ Standard Library, which provide a higher level of abstraction and are generally safer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ifstream` object from the C++ Standard Library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\nstd::ifstream file(path_max_link_width.c_str());\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: for the `ifstream` object\n- `<string>`: for the `string` object and its `c_str()` method\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c961bb23-193f-31f4-b8b6-c0cbe0103838",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, the source pointer may be null, which could also lead to undefined behavior. The use of memcpy in such cases is unsafe, as memcpy does not handle overlapping memory regions correctly. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this issue, and the function verdict confirms this as a genuine problem.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nAdd a runtime check to ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before performing the memory copy. This prevents undefined behavior when using memcpy.\n\n```cpp\nif ((metrics_table != &metrics_tbl) &&\n    ((char*)metrics_table + sizeof(amdsmi_hsmp_metrics_table_t) <= (char*)&metrics_tbl ||\n     (char*)&metrics_tbl + sizeof(amdsmi_hsmp_metrics_table_t) <= (char*)metrics_table)) {\n    std::memcpy(metrics_table, &metrics_tbl, sizeof(amdsmi_hsmp_metrics_table_t));\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case appropriately, e.g., use memmove or log an error\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible or cannot be ruled out, use std::memmove instead of std::memcpy. memmove is safe for overlapping regions and will prevent undefined behavior.\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(metrics_table, &metrics_tbl, sizeof(amdsmi_hsmp_metrics_table_t));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
