            {
              "id": "51972bad-11a3-33a2-a9c7-7b7177282a69",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to the potential for the destination pointer to be null at the point where the string copy operation is performed. The verdict is supported by the classification indicating undefined behavior risk and the explicit 'GENUINE' verdict. The source is a C-style string, which is generally safe, but the destination pointer may be null, and there are no visible checks to ensure it is valid before the operation. This can lead to a crash or other unpredictable behavior if the destination is indeed null. No explicit null-termination is enforced after the copy, and there is no guard to ensure the destination is non-null, increasing the risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the string copy operation, ensure that the destination pointer (`cpu_info`) and its `model_name` member are not null. This prevents undefined behavior if the pointer is invalid. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (cpu_info && cpu_info->model_name) {\n    strncpy(cpu_info->model_name, model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    cpu_info->model_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the destination is valid before copying, and also explicitly null-terminates the string to avoid potential non-terminated strings if the source is longer than the buffer.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the structure of `cpu_info` guarantees that `model_name` is always a valid buffer (for example, if it is a fixed-size array and `cpu_info` is always allocated), you may only need to check that `cpu_info` itself is not null:\n\n```cpp\nif (cpu_info) {\n    strncpy(cpu_info->model_name, model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    cpu_info->model_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach is suitable if you are certain that `model_name` cannot be null, but `cpu_info` might be.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 3\nIf you want to ensure robust error handling, you can add a check and return an error or handle the situation gracefully if the destination is null:\n\n```cpp\nif (!cpu_info || !cpu_info->model_name) {\n    // Handle error: destination is null\n    return ERROR_INVALID_POINTER;\n}\nstrncpy(cpu_info->model_name, model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\ncpu_info->model_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\nThis makes the function safer and more predictable in the presence of invalid pointers.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "37cdc37c-048b-35e7-9d93-b1aa2ebfb859",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in the memory copy operation. The verdict is marked as 'GENUINE', and the classification explicitly states a risk of undefined behavior. The overlap is confirmed by data flow analysis, which traced a possible path from the destination buffer to the source buffer, indicating that the two may refer to overlapping memory. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the use of memcpy in such a scenario can lead to unpredictable results or program crashes. The source and destination are both pointers, and the size calculation is not derived from a known safe capacity, further increasing the risk. Null pointer dereference is not feasible here, as both buffers are confirmed to be non-null before the call.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. You can add a runtime check to verify that the memory regions are distinct:\n\n```cpp\nif ((cpu_set + cpu_set_size <= bitmask.data()) || (bitmask.data() + cpu_set_size <= cpu_set)) {\n    std::memcpy(cpu_set, bitmask.data(), cpu_set_size * sizeof(uint64_t));\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case appropriately, e.g., use memmove or log an error\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the memory regions do not overlap before calling memcpy, preventing undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible or cannot be ruled out, replace the memcpy call with memmove, which is safe for overlapping regions:\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(cpu_set, bitmask.data(), cpu_set_size * sizeof(uint64_t));\n```\nThis change ensures correct behavior even if the source and destination buffers overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2fd20e28-e306-3773-9596-5e8a08501aaf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the source and destination do not overlap, and the source pointer may also be null, which further increases the risk. The destination pointer is guarded as non-null, but the lack of a null check for the source and the potential for overlap make this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add a runtime check to verify that the memory ranges are distinct. If overlap is possible, handle the case appropriately or avoid the copy.\n\n```c\nif ((vbios_version != info->version) &&\n    ((vbios_version + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= info->version) ||\n     (info->version + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= vbios_version))) {\n    strncpy(info->version, vbios_version, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case, e.g., use a temporary buffer or log an error\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be handled, use a function that supports overlapping regions, such as memmove, instead of strncpy. Note that memmove does not NUL-terminate strings, so you must handle NUL-termination manually if needed.\n\n```c\nmemmove(info->version, vbios_version, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n// Optionally ensure NUL-termination:\ninfo->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "13437bfe-bd00-3bcd-b8e7-a062cdad5011",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the strncpy function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, the source pointer may be null at runtime, which could also lead to a crash or further undefined behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent these issues, and the destination buffer's capacity is not clearly defined or enforced. The destination is confirmed to be non-null before the call, but the source is not. These factors indicate a real risk that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. You can add a runtime check to compare the addresses and sizes of the buffers. If overlap is possible, handle the case appropriately, such as by using a temporary buffer or by aborting the operation.\n\n```cpp\nif ((reinterpret_cast<const void *>(info->version) + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= reinterpret_cast<const void *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str)) ||\n    (reinterpret_cast<const void *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str) + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= reinterpret_cast<const void *>(info->version))) {\n    strncpy(info->version, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap: use a temporary buffer or log an error\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlap between source and destination cannot be ruled out, use the `memmove` function instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Note that `memmove` does not guarantee null-termination, so you may need to add it manually if required.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->version, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n// Optionally ensure null-termination if info->version is a string\ninfo->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
