            {
              "id": "6420b844-b1a1-3469-bf3d-8f41319de2d7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. The source pointer may also be null, which could cause a crash. The destination is guarded as non-null, but the combination of possible overlap and lack of null-termination makes this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling strncpy. You can add a runtime check to verify that the memory regions are distinct:\n\n```cpp\nif ((info->part_number + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn)) ||\n    (reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn) + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= info->part_number)) {\n    strncpy(info->part_number, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->part_number[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap error, e.g., log or use memmove if safe\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the memory regions do not overlap. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlap between source and destination is possible or cannot be ruled out, use memmove instead of strncpy, as memmove is safe for overlapping memory regions:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->part_number, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_pn), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\ninfo->part_number[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\nThis approach avoids undefined behavior even if the buffers overlap. Always ensure the destination is null-terminated after the copy.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
