            {
              "id": "575ef44a-f2da-33c7-b357-78915bf2cf8b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the C standard for strncpy and can result in unpredictable program behavior. The risk is further increased because the analysis could not rule out the possibility that the source pointer is null, although the destination is guarded as non-null. The count argument is a macro minus one, which is a common pattern but does not mitigate the overlap risk. An explicit null-terminator is written after the call, which is good practice, but does not address the core issue of potential memory overlap. Therefore, this is a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be remediated.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nAdd a runtime check to ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before calling `strncpy`. This can be done by comparing the addresses and lengths of the buffers. If overlap is detected, handle the case appropriately (e.g., skip the copy, log an error, or use a safe alternative).\n\n```cpp\nif ((info->build_date + (AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1) <= reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date)) ||\n    (reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date) + (AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1) <= info->build_date)) {\n    strncpy(info->build_date, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    info->build_date[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap: log error, use memmove, or skip copy\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlap between source and destination is possible or cannot be ruled out, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is defined for overlapping memory regions. Note that `memmove` does not null-terminate, so you must handle null-termination explicitly.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->build_date, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\ninfo->build_date[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
