            {
              "id": "a6cfabca-f3ca-3ce8-8270-e6f1abbdd75f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the strncpy function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. The source pointer may also be null, which could cause a crash. The destination is guarded as non-null, reducing risk in that direction. The use of a constant for the copy length does not guarantee safety if the actual buffer sizes are not properly managed. These factors indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when using `strncpy`, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add a runtime check before the copy to verify that the memory ranges are distinct:\n\n```cpp\nif ((info->name + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.name)) ||\n    (reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.name) + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH <= info->name)) {\n    strncpy(info->name, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.name), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap error, e.g., log or use safe copy\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the source and destination do not overlap. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after the copy to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlap between source and destination cannot be ruled out, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is safe for overlapping memory regions. After copying, ensure the destination is null-terminated:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->name, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.name), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\ninfo->name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior even if the memory regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
