            {
              "id": "aff4f22c-8635-3534-9d43-33bdc7de302c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the strncpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the strncpy function and can lead to unpredictable program behavior or security issues. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the source is not null, which further increases the risk. The destination is guarded as non-null, but the source is not. No explicit null-termination is performed after the copy, which is another potential issue, but the primary concern here is the memory overlap. This is a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. You can add a runtime check to verify that the memory ranges are disjoint. For example:\n\n```c\nif ((brand < info->vram_vendor || brand >= info->vram_vendor + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH) && brand != NULL) {\n    strncpy(info->vram_vendor, brand, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->vram_vendor[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the source pointer does not fall within the destination buffer's range and that the source is not null. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf you cannot guarantee that the source and destination do not overlap, use the `memmove` function instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is safe for overlapping memory regions. For example:\n\n```c\nif (brand != NULL) {\n    size_t len = strnlen(brand, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    memmove(info->vram_vendor, brand, len);\n    info->vram_vendor[len] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach safely handles overlapping memory and ensures null-termination. Note that `memmove` does not null-terminate, so you must do so explicitly.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
