            {
              "id": "0c3f9dc9-a2e1-3d7c-92c3-292b68af3001",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will keep reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(schema_key)` could potentially lead to a buffer overflow if `schema_key` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Alternatively, consider using safer string handling functions that take the size of the buffer as an argument, such as `strnlen`, which will not read past the specified length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure schema_key is null-terminated\nschema_key[schema_key.size()] = '\\0';\nfeature_line.substr(strlen(schema_key)).c_str();\n```\n\nOr use `strnlen`:\n\n```cpp\nfeature_line.substr(strnlen(schema_key, schema_key.size())).c_str();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` and `std::string::substr`\n- `<cstring>`: for `std::strlen`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4bcffff6-2591-3fec-84ca-559766c225d2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(version_key)` could potentially lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability if `version_key` is not null-terminated.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` that take a maximum length parameter to prevent buffer overflows, such as `strnlen`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\n// Ensure version_key is null-terminated\nversion_key[version_key.size() - 1] = '\\0';\n\n// Use strnlen instead of strlen\nfeature_line.substr(strnlen(version_key, version_key.size())).c_str();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::string` and its member functions.\n- `<cstring>`: for `std::strlen` and `std::strnlen`.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0f050bab-d8f8-3792-9fa3-e3c6dba22c10",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk: the destination buffer's capacity is unknown, and the length argument used in the copy operation is not proven to be safe. The destination is a character pointer, and there is no evidence that the length argument is derived from the actual buffer size. Additionally, there are no explicit checks or guards to ensure that the length does not exceed the buffer's capacity, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy. The source is a C-style string, which is generally safe from overlap issues, and both the source and destination are not null at this point. However, the lack of buffer size validation and null-termination makes this a high-confidence, actionable issue that could lead to buffer overflows or non-null-terminated strings.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo mitigate the risk of buffer overflow and ensure proper null-termination, always validate that the length argument does not exceed the actual size of the destination buffer. If the size of the destination buffer is known (for example, as a macro or constant), use it directly in the copy operation and ensure the buffer is null-terminated after copying. For example:\n\n```cpp\n// Assume NAME_BUF_SIZE is the actual size of 'name'\nstrncpy(name, socket->get_socket_id().c_str(), NAME_BUF_SIZE - 1);\nname[NAME_BUF_SIZE - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis approach ensures that the buffer is not overrun and is always null-terminated, preventing potential string-related vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the size of the destination buffer is not known at this location, consider refactoring the code to pass the buffer size as an additional argument, or use safer string handling functions such as snprintf, which always null-terminates the buffer:\n\n```cpp\n// Assume NAME_BUF_SIZE is the actual size of 'name'\nsnprintf(name, NAME_BUF_SIZE, \"%s\", socket->get_socket_id().c_str());\n```\n\nThis ensures that the buffer is not overrun and is always null-terminated.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8b953c96-84da-32f3-a412-7b2b8870a2c8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in the call to the string copy function. The destination is a character pointer, and the source is an identifier, with dataflow analysis indicating that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions. There is no explicit check to ensure that the source and destination do not overlap, and the function used does not handle overlapping memory safely. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the copy, and the source pointer may be null at this point, which could also lead to undefined behavior. These factors make this a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent potential memory corruption or crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. Add a runtime check to verify that the memory regions are distinct. If overlap is possible, handle the copy differently or avoid it entirely.\n\n```c\nif ((name + len <= proc_id) || (proc_id + len <= name)) {\n    strncpy(name, proc_id, len);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination\n    if (len > 0) {\n        name[len - 1] = '\\0';\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case appropriately, e.g., use memmove or log an error\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlap between source and destination cannot be ruled out, use a memory-safe function that supports overlapping regions, such as memmove, instead of strncpy. This will prevent undefined behavior.\n\n```c\nmemmove(name, proc_id, len);\n// Optionally, ensure null-termination\nif (len > 0) {\n    name[len - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e6c1ef5d-5a8d-36c8-ae99-94952fcaca2a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the code copies data from a string to a character buffer using a length argument that is decremented by one (len-1), but there is no check to ensure that the length is greater than zero before performing the operation. If the length is zero, subtracting one will result in a very large value due to unsigned integer underflow, potentially causing a buffer overflow. The source is a C-style string, and the destination is a character pointer, which increases the risk if the pointer is not properly managed. There are no explicit null-terminations after the copy, and no guard is present to prevent underflow. The classification and verdict both indicate a high likelihood of a real issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data, ensure that the length argument is greater than zero to prevent unsigned integer underflow. Add a check to verify that the length is at least 1 before performing the copy. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nif (len > 0) {\n    strncpy(name, vendor_name.c_str(), len - 1);\n    name[len - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the copy only occurs when the length is valid and that the destination buffer is always null-terminated, preventing buffer overflows and string-related vulnerabilities.\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2848d831-a937-3fec-bcab-5526797940b7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The verdict is supported by the classification indicating undefined behavior risk and the explicit 'GENUINE' verdict. The analysis determined that overlap is feasible based on data flow between the source and destination, which can cause issues with functions like memcpy that do not support overlapping regions. Additionally, the source may be null at this call site, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior. There are no explicit guards or checks to prevent these issues, and the destination is not a pointer-based member, making it less likely to be null but not eliminating the overlap risk. Developers should address both the potential for overlapping memory and the possibility of a null source pointer to ensure safe and correct operation.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between regions that may overlap, add a runtime check to ensure the source and destination do not overlap before calling the memory copy function. If overlap is possible, use a safe alternative or handle the copy in a way that avoids undefined behavior.\n\n```cpp\nif ((char*)&header_value + sizeof(metrics_table_header_t) <= (char*)&tmp_header_info ||\n    (char*)&tmp_header_info + sizeof(metrics_table_header_t) <= (char*)&header_value) {\n    std::memcpy(&header_value, &tmp_header_info, sizeof(metrics_table_header_t));\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap safely, e.g., use memmove or copy via a temporary buffer\n    std::memmove(&header_value, &tmp_header_info, sizeof(metrics_table_header_t));\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, replace the memory copy function with a function that is safe for overlapping memory regions, such as memmove. This ensures correct behavior regardless of whether the source and destination overlap.\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(&header_value, &tmp_header_info, sizeof(metrics_table_header_t));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "31bd3edb-d331-3f1d-a44d-ef649b2d147e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in the call to the string copy function. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory regions, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. The risk is further confirmed by the verdict indicating a genuine issue. There are no indications that the source or destination pointers are null at this point, and the code does not appear to have explicit checks to prevent overlap. The use of a length argument that is one less than a maximum macro is common, but does not mitigate the overlap risk. An explicit null-terminator is written after the copy, which is good practice, but does not address the core issue of overlapping memory regions.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the string copy, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. You can add a runtime check to compare the memory ranges of the source and destination. If overlap is possible, handle the case appropriately (e.g., skip the copy, use a temporary buffer, or log an error):\n\n```cpp\nif ((market_name + (AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1) <= name) || (name + (AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1) <= market_name)) {\n    std::strncpy(market_name, name, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    market_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap: use a temporary buffer or log an error\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be handled, use a function that is safe for overlapping buffers, such as `memmove`, instead of the standard string copy function. Note that `memmove` does not guarantee null-termination, so you must add it explicitly:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(market_name, name, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\nmarket_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "24db3132-e3c0-3d5e-8dcb-464f13f790a4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflow, etc. In this case, the function `std::localtime` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to data races if used in a multi-threaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions in a multi-threaded context. Instead, use their thread-safe counterparts. In this case, `std::localtime` should be replaced with `std::localtime_s` (on Windows) or `localtime_r` (on POSIX systems).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <ctime>\n\nstd::time_t timer;\nstd::tm bt = {};\n\n#if defined(_WIN32) || defined(_WIN64)\n    localtime_s(&bt, &timer);\n#else\n    localtime_r(&timer, &bt);\n#endif\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- `<ctime>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "85a292bd-e28e-3713-9212-75aa9edc1ff7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strcpy(vbios_version, GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA)`, the `strcpy` function is used to copy the `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` string into the `vbios_version` buffer. If the `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` string is larger than the `vbios_version` buffer, it can cause a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions require you to specify the maximum number of characters to copy, preventing buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with the `strncpy` function, and ensure that the size argument is the size of the destination buffer. For example:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(vbios_version, GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA, sizeof(vbios_version));\nvbios_version[sizeof(vbios_version) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3baba8f8-759a-3970-a5ba-39e8570654b0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk due to the destination buffer's capacity being unknown and the copy length not being proven safe. The destination is a character pointer, and there is no evidence that its size is sufficient for the specified copy length. The copy length is determined by a macro minus one, but there is no guarantee that the destination buffer is at least as large as this value. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. The source is a C-style string, which is generally safe, and there is no risk of overlapping memory or null pointers at this call site. However, the lack of buffer size validation and null-termination makes this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying data, ensure that the destination buffer is at least as large as the maximum copy length. If the size of the destination buffer is not known, add a check or assert to validate its size. After copying, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\n// Example fix:\nassert(market_name != nullptr);\n// Ensure market_name points to a buffer of at least AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH bytes\nstd::strncpy(market_name, empty.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\nmarket_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis ensures that the buffer is not overflowed and is always null-terminated, preventing potential string-related vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf you cannot guarantee the size of the destination buffer, consider using safer string handling functions or dynamic allocation. Alternatively, use C++ string objects to manage memory automatically and avoid manual buffer management.\n\n```cpp\n// Example fix using std::string:\nstd::string market_name_str = empty;\n// If you must use a char* buffer, copy safely:\nstrncpy(market_name, market_name_str.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\nmarket_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis approach leverages C++ string safety and still provides a safe copy to a character buffer if needed.\n\n## In Context Remediation 3\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "036665bd-f247-356c-9339-825429e7e8c7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of a malloc call is used as the destination in a strcpy operation immediately after allocation, without any check to ensure that the allocation succeeded (i.e., that the returned pointer is not NULL). There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (strcpy) that will dereference the pointer. The absence of a null check before use makes this a genuine risk for null pointer dereference, especially if malloc fails due to memory exhaustion. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is also returned and passed to another call, which increases the impact of a potential failure.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., return an error code, log the failure, or clean up resources). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nvbios_version = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nstrcpy(vbios_version, src);\n```\n\nA safe remediation would be:\n\n```c\nvbios_version = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nif (vbios_version == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure: log, return error, or clean up\n    return ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY;\n}\nstrcpy(vbios_version, src);\n```\n\nThis ensures that strcpy is only called if the allocation succeeded, preventing a possible null pointer dereference. For more information, see:\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ff287b49-e4bd-317d-895e-4f8cc55775f3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause a program to crash or, in the worst case, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions can also be misused, so it's important to ensure that the size argument correctly represents the size of the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function, such as `strncpy`. Here's how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(gpu_vendor_name, GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA, sizeof(gpu_vendor_name));\ngpu_vendor_name[sizeof(gpu_vendor_name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nThis code will copy at most `sizeof(gpu_vendor_name)` characters from `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` to `gpu_vendor_name`, and then ensure that `gpu_vendor_name` is null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e60adcc9-a0be-3b2b-8d85-28095efe06bb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strcpy(dev_name, GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA)`, the `strcpy` function is used to copy the string `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` into the `dev_name` buffer. If `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` is larger than `dev_name`, this can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions require you to specify the maximum number of characters to copy, preventing buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix using `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(dev_name, GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA, sizeof(dev_name) - 1);\ndev_name[sizeof(dev_name) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy at most `sizeof(dev_name) - 1` characters from `GOAMDSMI_STRING_NA` to `dev_name`, and then manually null-terminate `dev_name` to ensure it is a valid string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fa8d34f5-b08f-30bb-886f-bccd30aa8e64",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of malloc is used as the destination in a strcpy call immediately after allocation, without any check for a NULL return value. There is no zero guard or overflow guard present, and the first use is a memory operation (strcpy) that will dereference the pointer. The absence of a null guard before use increases the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The presence of a cast does not mitigate this risk. The pointer is also returned and passed to another call, but this does not affect the immediate risk. Based on these factors, this is a genuine vulnerability with a low probability of being a false positive.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a null pointer dereference after memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\ngpu_vendor_name = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nstrcpy(gpu_vendor_name, src);\n```\n\nYou should add a null check immediately after the allocation:\n\n```c\ngpu_vendor_name = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nif (gpu_vendor_name == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return an error or set an error code\n    return NULL;\n}\nstrcpy(gpu_vendor_name, src);\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would lead to undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6dd7f938-a4ed-3f9d-8c72-ba0e18fc7a00",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of malloc is used as the destination in a strcpy call immediately after allocation, without any check for a NULL return value. The absence of a null guard before the first use increases the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The allocation size is not a simple literal or sizeof(T) pattern, and there are no zero or overflow guards present. The pointer is returned and passed to another call, but these do not mitigate the immediate risk. The presence of a cast does not affect the vulnerability. All these factors indicate a genuine risk of null dereference after failed allocation.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., return an error code, log the failure, or clean up resources). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nchar *dev_name = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nstrcpy(dev_name, src);\n```\n\nA safe remediation would be:\n\n```c\nchar *dev_name = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nif (dev_name == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure (e.g., return error, log, etc.)\n    return NULL;\n}\nstrcpy(dev_name, src);\n```\n\nThis ensures that strcpy is only called if the allocation succeeded, preventing a possible null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2894f732-a6ff-33d9-8b98-9e4fdd37b99d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. These functions are often prohibited because they do not perform bounds checking or other necessary runtime checks, making them susceptible to exploitation.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_2)` is considered as a vulnerability sink. However, without more context, it's hard to provide a specific reason why this function could be insecure. It might be due to the fact that it enables a high level of debugging which could expose sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this type of vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n- Avoid using insecure or unsafe C library functions.\n- Use secure alternatives to these functions that perform necessary runtime checks.\n- Always validate and sanitize input data.\n- Implement proper error handling.\n- Regularly update and patch your software.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to provide a specific fix for the provided code snippet. However, if the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_2)` is insecure because it enables a high level of debugging, you might want to consider using a lower debug level or disabling debugging in the production environment.\n\n```cpp\n// Instead of\nenable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_2);\n\n// Consider using\nenable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to provide a specific list of library dependencies required by the provided code snippet.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3ebbea98-2062-3357-9731-7ae5eeb22603",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow or underflow if the input string represents a number outside the range of representable integer values.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that include proper error handling and boundary checks. For `atoi()`, a safer alternative is `strtol()` or `sscanf()`, which provide error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `strtol()` or `sscanf()`. Here is an example of how to use `strtol()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <cerrno>\n\nchar *endptr;\nlong ev_ret = strtol(ev_str, &endptr, 10);\n\nif (endptr == ev_str) {\n    // handle error: no digits were found\n} else if ((ev_ret == LONG_MIN || ev_ret == LONG_MAX) && errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle error: the number is out of the range of representable values\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `strtol()`\n- `<cerrno>`: for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c380535e-31c8-312a-bef2-f07ba904f5cf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a program uses a function that can lead to a security risk. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow or underflow. This function does not perform any error checking, so it can lead to undefined behavior if the input string cannot be converted to an integer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using the `atoi()` function. Instead, use functions that perform error checking, such as `strtol()`, `sscanf()`, or `stoi()` (for C++11 and later). These functions will allow you to handle errors more gracefully and avoid undefined behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `strtol()`, `sscanf()`, or `stoi()`. Here is an example of how you can use `strtol()`:\n\n```cpp\nchar *end;\nlong ret = strtol(ev_str, &end, 10);\nif (end == ev_str || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `#include <stdlib.h>` for `strtol()`\n- `#include <errno.h>` for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-681: Incorrect Conversion between Numeric Types](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/681.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b12109dd-0a3a-3623-ae83-27d5e200f508",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other security issues. These functions are often part of the C Standard Library and are included for backward compatibility, but their use is discouraged in modern programming.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is identified as a vulnerability sink. However, without more context, it's hard to provide a specific mitigation strategy. This function seems to be a custom function, not a standard C or C++ function. If this function is using prohibited C functions internally, it could lead to potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace the unsafe functions with safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other safety measures. If the function `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is using unsafe functions internally, you should revise its implementation to use safer alternatives.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout knowing the internal implementation of `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)`, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if it's using unsafe C functions, you should replace them with safer alternatives. For example, if it's using `strcpy`, you could replace it with `strncpy` and make sure to null-terminate the string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nWithout more context, it's hard to determine the specific library dependencies required by the code example. However, if `enable_debug_level(GOAMDSMI_DEBUG_LEVEL_1)` is a function from a specific library, that library would be a dependency.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible at all times."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "17a0f40a-2e6f-3df8-89a9-ef7793a9a277",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a likely vulnerability due to a missing check that the length parameter is greater than zero. The code copies data using a function that takes a length argument, but there is no guard to ensure that the length is positive. If the length is zero, subtracting one results in a very large value due to underflow, which can cause a buffer overflow or memory corruption. The destination buffer is a character pointer, and the source is a C-style string, which does not mitigate the risk. There are no explicit null-terminations or other safety measures present. The analysis did not detect any risk of overlapping memory regions or null pointers for source or destination, but the lack of a guard for the length parameter is a critical issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore calling the copy function, ensure that the length parameter is greater than zero and does not exceed the capacity of the destination buffer. Add an explicit check to prevent underflow and buffer overflow:\n\n```cpp\nif (len > 0 && len <= MAX_VERSION_SIZE) {\n    std::strncpy(version, empty.c_str(), len-1);\n    version[len-1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nReplace `MAX_VERSION_SIZE` with the actual size of the destination buffer if known. This prevents the length from underflowing and ensures the buffer is properly null-terminated.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if the size of the destination buffer is known, use it directly to avoid relying on an external length parameter:\n\n```cpp\nconstexpr size_t VERSION_SIZE = 64; // Example size\nstd::strncpy(version, empty.c_str(), VERSION_SIZE - 1);\nversion[VERSION_SIZE - 1] = '\\0';\n```\nThis approach eliminates the risk of underflow and ensures the buffer is always null-terminated.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2a44b698-f2c8-347c-8d06-3d625583e114",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a likely vulnerability due to a missing check that the length parameter is greater than zero. The code copies data from a C-style string into a character buffer using a length argument that is decremented by one (sizeOfBuff-1), but there is no check to ensure that sizeOfBuff is greater than zero. If sizeOfBuff is zero, the subtraction results in a very large value due to unsigned integer underflow, potentially causing a buffer overflow. The destination buffer is a pointer, and there is no evidence that the length is derived from the actual buffer capacity. Although the source is a C-style string and there is no risk of overlapping memory or null pointers at this call site, the absence of a guard makes this a real risk. An explicit null terminator is written after the copy, but this does not mitigate the risk of copying excessive data if the length is not properly checked.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying data into the buffer, ensure that the length parameter (sizeOfBuff) is greater than zero. This prevents unsigned integer underflow when subtracting one from the length, which could otherwise result in a very large value being passed to the copy function and cause a buffer overflow.\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeOfBuff > 0) {\n    std::strncpy(buff, contents.c_str(), sizeOfBuff-1);\n    buff[sizeOfBuff-1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis check ensures that the copy operation only occurs when the buffer size is valid, preventing both underflow and overflow conditions.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, use a safer approach by explicitly checking the buffer size and using std::strncpy only when the size is valid. If the buffer size is zero, avoid the copy entirely to prevent undefined behavior.\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeOfBuff > 0) {\n    std::strncpy(buff, contents.c_str(), sizeOfBuff-1);\n    buff[sizeOfBuff-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle error: buffer size is invalid\n}\n```\n\nThis approach also allows you to handle the error case explicitly, improving the robustness of your code.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            }
